      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Convolution: The Arithmetic of Overlap\"\n",
        "description: \"Why the same operation counts dice sums, multiplies polynomials, blurs images, and lets Fourier transforms turn a slow sliding calculation into fast pointwise multiplication.\"\n",
        "date: 2026-06-18\n",
        "categories: [pure, applied, probability, computer-science, algorithms, analysis]\n",
        "bibliography: ../../../references.bib\n",
        "draft: true\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.epigraph}\n",
        "*\"Mathematics is the art of giving the same name to different things.\"*\n",
        "\n",
        "[-- Henri Poincare, traditionally attributed]{.attribution}\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "There is a small arithmetic fact that every board game player learns before they\n",
        "know they have learned any probability: rolling a $7$ with two dice feels common,\n",
        "and rolling a $2$ or $12$ feels rare.\n",
        "\n",
        "That fact is not mysterious. There is only one way to roll a $2$, namely\n",
        "$(1,1)$. There are six ways to roll a $7$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(1,6),\\ (2,5),\\ (3,4),\\ (4,3),\\ (5,2),\\ (6,1).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "What is worth noticing is the shape of the count. The numbers climb, peak, and\n",
        "fall:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1,\\ 2,\\ 3,\\ 4,\\ 5,\\ 6,\\ 5,\\ 4,\\ 3,\\ 2,\\ 1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Now here is the jump. The same operation that produces this little pyramid also\n",
        "explains how a camera blurs an image, how a signal picks up an echo, how two\n",
        "polynomials multiply, how independent random variables add, and why the fast\n",
        "Fourier transform is one of the great algorithmic shortcuts of modern computing.\n",
        "\n",
        "That operation is **convolution**.\n",
        "\n",
        "I first learned to *see* this operation from Grant Sanderson's 3Blue1Brown video\n",
        "on the subject [@sanderson2022convolutions]. This post is my attempt to rebuild\n",
        "the idea in prose, slowly enough that the formula feels less like a definition\n",
        "and more like a thing you were already doing with the dice.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The question convolution answers\n",
        "\n",
        "Let us strip the dice story down to its bones.\n",
        "\n",
        "One die contributes a number. The other die contributes a number. We care about\n",
        "their sum. So for a target total, say $7$, we ask:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Which pairs of contributions land on this same target?\n",
        "\n",
        "That is the whole question.\n",
        "\n",
        "For two dice, every face is equally likely, so each valid pair contributes the\n",
        "same weight. Counting pairs is enough. But imagine the dice are loaded. Maybe the\n",
        "first die likes small numbers and the second die likes large numbers. Then the\n",
        "sum $7$ is not merely \"six ways\"; each way carries a weight:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X=1)P(Y=6),\\quad P(X=2)P(Y=5),\\quad \\ldots,\\quad P(X=6)P(Y=1).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "The probability of $X+Y=7$ is the sum of all those weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "That sentence is convolution in its most honest form:\n",
        "\n",
        "> To find the weight at a target, look at all pairs that combine to hit the\n",
        "> target, multiply their individual weights, and add.\n",
        "\n",
        "If $a_i$ is the weight of the first contribution being $i$, and $b_j$ is the\n",
        "weight of the second contribution being $j$, then the weight of total $k$ is\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(a*b)_k = \\sum_i a_i b_{k-i}.\n",
        "$$ {#eq-conv}\n",
        "\n",
        "The symbol $*$ is pronounced \"convolved with.\" The subscript $k-i$ is not a\n",
        "trick. It is simply the complement: if the first contribution is $i$, the second\n",
        "one must be $k-i$ for the pair to land on $k$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The formula looks like a wall until you read it as a sentence:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Fix the target $k$. Try every first move $i$. The second move is then forced\n",
        "> to be $k-i$. Multiply the two weights. Add over all $i$.\n",
        "\n",
        "That is all.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Watching the slide\n",
        "\n",
        "There is a visual move that makes @eq-conv feel inevitable. Write the first list\n",
        "of weights in place. Write the second list backwards. Now slide the reversed\n",
        "list past the first one. At each position, multiply the overlapping entries and\n",
        "add them.\n",
        "\n",
        "Why reverse one list? Because the second index is $k-i$. As $i$ increases, the\n",
        "complement $k-i$ decreases. One hand moves right while the other moves left. The\n",
        "reversal is not a convention; it is the geometry of addition.\n",
        "\n",
        "For two fair dice, the lists are both\n",
        "$$\n",
        "[1,1,1,1,1,1],\n",
        "$$\n",
        "if we are only counting ways. Slide one copy past the other. First only one pair\n",
        "overlaps. Then two pairs. Then three, four, five, six. Then the overlap shrinks\n",
        "again. The pyramid was not a probability miracle. It was the length of the\n",
        "overlap window."
      ],
      "id": "cd7ac122"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def convolve(a, b):\n",
        "    out = [0] * (len(a) + len(b) - 1)\n",
        "    for i, ai in enumerate(a):\n",
        "        for j, bj in enumerate(b):\n",
        "            out[i + j] += ai * bj\n",
        "    return out\n",
        "\n",
        "die = [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]\n",
        "counts = convolve(die, die)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(counts)\n",
        "print(sum(counts))"
      ],
      "id": "060744a6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The output has total mass $36$, one count for each ordered pair of die rolls. If\n",
        "we divide by $36$, those counts become probabilities."
      ],
      "id": "1dc93c81"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "probabilities = [c / 36 for c in counts]\n",
        "for total, p in zip(range(2, 13), probabilities):\n",
        "    print(f\"{total:2d}: {p:.3f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "c633e75f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Nothing in that code knows about dice. It knows only one rule: combine all pairs\n",
        "whose indices add to the same target. The dice merely gave the rule a friendly\n",
        "face.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Loaded dice, same machine\n",
        "\n",
        "Let us make the dice unfair, because unfairness reveals what the multiplication\n",
        "in @eq-conv is doing.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the first die prefers low numbers and the second prefers high numbers.\n",
        "Then a total can become likely for two different reasons: many pairs may hit it,\n",
        "or a few especially heavy pairs may hit it. Convolution does not care which\n",
        "story wins. It adds the weights of all compatible stories."
      ],
      "id": "08e0c7f2"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "low_die = [6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]\n",
        "high_die = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\n",
        "\n",
        "total_weight = sum(low_die) * sum(high_die)\n",
        "weighted = convolve(low_die, high_die)\n",
        "\n",
        "for total, weight in zip(range(2, 13), weighted):\n",
        "    print(f\"{total:2d}: {weight / total_weight:.3f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "0e9bb235",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This is why convolution is the natural operation for adding independent random\n",
        "variables. The word \"independent\" is exactly what lets the weight of a pair\n",
        "factor as\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X=i,\\ Y=j)=P(X=i)P(Y=j).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Once that multiplication is legal, summing over all pairs with $i+j=k$ gives the\n",
        "distribution of $X+Y$.\n",
        "\n",
        "So convolution is not an arbitrary new operation. It is what ordinary addition\n",
        "of uncertain quantities looks like when you keep every possible way the target\n",
        "could have happened.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The same operation hiding in polynomials\n",
        "\n",
        "Now put probability aside and multiply two polynomials:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2)(b_0+b_1x+b_2x^2).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where does the coefficient of $x^3$ come from? It comes from every pair of terms\n",
        "whose exponents add to $3$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a_1b_2x^3 + a_2b_1x^3.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "More generally, the coefficient of $x^k$ in\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A(x)B(x)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "is\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sum_i a_i b_{k-i}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "That is the same formula again.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is one of those moments where mathematics earns Poincare's line in the\n",
        "epigraph. Adding random variables and multiplying polynomials look unrelated at\n",
        "the surface. One talks about probability; the other talks about algebra. But\n",
        "underneath, both ask for the same bookkeeping:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Which pairs of indices add to this target?\n",
        "\n",
        "That is why probability generating functions work. If\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A(x)=\\sum_i P(X=i)x^i,\\qquad B(x)=\\sum_j P(Y=j)x^j,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "then\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A(x)B(x)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "is the generating function for $X+Y$. Polynomial multiplication is doing the\n",
        "probability convolution for you."
      ],
      "id": "3f426c55"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def poly_mul(a, b):\n",
        "    return convolve(a, b)\n",
        "\n",
        "# (1 + 2x + 3x^2)(4 + 5x + 6x^2)\n",
        "A = [1, 2, 3]\n",
        "B = [4, 5, 6]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(poly_mul(A, B))  # coefficients of 4 + 13x + 28x^2 + 27x^3 + 18x^4"
      ],
      "id": "d6438a01",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "It is worth pausing here. A single operation has now worn two costumes:\n",
        "\n",
        "- in probability, it adds independent quantities;\n",
        "- in algebra, it multiplies polynomials.\n",
        "\n",
        "The shared mechanism is not a metaphor. It is the same sum.\n",
        "\n",
        "## From lists to functions\n",
        "\n",
        "So far our objects have been finite lists. But the same picture survives when\n",
        "the lists become functions.\n",
        "\n",
        "Imagine two functions $f$ and $g$ on the real line. We want a new function whose\n",
        "value at $t$ gathers every way to split $t$ into two pieces:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "t = \\tau + (t-\\tau).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "The first function pays weight $f(\\tau)$ for the first piece. The second pays\n",
        "weight $g(t-\\tau)$ for the complement. Multiply those weights and add over every\n",
        "possible split. Since the splits are continuous, \"add\" becomes \"integrate\":\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(f*g)(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(\\tau)g(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau.\n",
        "$$ {#eq-cont-conv}\n",
        "\n",
        "That is continuous convolution.\n",
        "\n",
        "Again, do not let the integral intimidate you. It says the same thing @eq-conv\n",
        "said:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Fix the target. Try every first contribution. The second contribution is\n",
        "> forced. Multiply. Add.\n",
        "\n",
        "If the functions live in time and nothing happens before $t=0$, the natural\n",
        "version is the causal convolution\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(f*g)(t)=\\int_0^t f(\\tau)g(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau.\n",
        "$$ {#eq-causal-conv}\n",
        "\n",
        "That formula is the quiet engine under many linear systems. If $g$ is the\n",
        "system's response to a tiny impulse, and $f$ is the input signal, then the output\n",
        "at time $t$ is built by decomposing the input into all its earlier impulses:\n",
        "the impulse at time $\\tau$ contributes $f(\\tau)$, then the system has $t-\\tau$\n",
        "units of time to respond. Add all those echoes, and you get the output.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is where convolution reaches back toward the Laplace-transform story from\n",
        "the previous post. Linear time-invariant systems are systems where shifting an\n",
        "input in time merely shifts the response, and where inputs add cleanly. Those two\n",
        "properties almost force convolution as the input-output rule. In engineering\n",
        "language, if $h$ is the impulse response and $u$ is the input, the output is\n",
        "$$\n",
        "y = h*u.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Convolution is not a decoration on the theory. It is the arithmetic of linear\n",
        "memory.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why Fourier transforms love convolution\n",
        "\n",
        "The reason convolution becomes algorithmically powerful is that it is expensive\n",
        "in its native form. For two length-$n$ lists, the direct sliding computation\n",
        "checks about $n^2$ pairs. That is fine for dice and painful for long signals.\n",
        "\n",
        "Fourier analysis changes the room in which the calculation happens.\n",
        "\n",
        "There is a theorem, usually called the **convolution theorem**, which says:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat{f*g}=\\widehat f \\cdot \\widehat g.\n",
        "$$ {#eq-convolution-theorem}\n",
        "\n",
        "Read it in words:\n",
        "\n",
        "> In the Fourier world, convolution becomes pointwise multiplication.\n",
        "\n",
        "This should rhyme with the Laplace post. There, differentiation became\n",
        "multiplication by $s$. Here, convolution becomes multiplication frequency by\n",
        "frequency. In both cases, a messy operation in the original world becomes a\n",
        "simple operation after a transform.\n",
        "\n",
        "That is the deeper pattern:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Original world | Transform world |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| differentiate | multiply by frequency / $s$ |\n",
        "| convolve | multiply pointwise |\n",
        "\n",
        "Why should convolution turn into multiplication? A good intuition is that\n",
        "Fourier modes are the pure tones of translation. A sine wave, shifted in time,\n",
        "does not become some new species of function; it remains the same sine wave,\n",
        "only with its phase changed. Since convolution is built from shifted copies, the\n",
        "Fourier basis is exactly the coordinate system where those shifts become simple\n",
        "scalings. In the right coordinates, the sliding machine diagonalizes.\n",
        "\n",
        "That last word is not accidental. If you have read the spectral theorem post,\n",
        "you have seen this move before. A hard linear operation becomes easy when we\n",
        "write it in the directions it naturally stretches. Fourier analysis is the\n",
        "spectral theorem for translation-shaped problems.\n",
        "\n",
        "The computational payoff is the fast Fourier transform. Instead of doing the\n",
        "sliding sum directly, one can:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. transform both lists,\n",
        "2. multiply corresponding Fourier coefficients,\n",
        "3. transform back.\n",
        "\n",
        "The direct convolution costs about $n^2$ operations. The FFT route costs about\n",
        "$n\\log n$ operations [@oppenheim1999discrete]. That difference is why\n",
        "convolution can be everywhere at industrial scale: audio filters, image blur,\n",
        "large integer multiplication, polynomial arithmetic, probability distributions,\n",
        "and the convolutional layers that gave early deep learning systems their eyes.\n",
        "\n",
        "## A small checksum\n",
        "\n",
        "It is useful to end with a piece of code that has no philosophy in it. We will\n",
        "compare two ways to multiply polynomials: direct convolution and a symbolic\n",
        "expansion written by hand for a small example."
      ],
      "id": "40f67dc3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def pretty_poly(coeffs):\n",
        "    terms = []\n",
        "    for k, c in enumerate(coeffs):\n",
        "        if c == 0:\n",
        "            continue\n",
        "        if k == 0:\n",
        "            terms.append(str(c))\n",
        "        elif k == 1:\n",
        "            terms.append(f\"{c}x\")\n",
        "        else:\n",
        "            terms.append(f\"{c}x^{k}\")\n",
        "    return \" + \".join(terms)\n",
        "\n",
        "A = [2, -1, 3]       # 2 - x + 3x^2\n",
        "B = [1, 4]           # 1 + 4x\n",
        "\n",
        "C = convolve(A, B)\n",
        "print(pretty_poly(C))"
      ],
      "id": "482892d5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The coefficient of $x^2$ is $-4+3=-1$, because there are exactly two ways to\n",
        "make exponent $2$: choose $x$ from the first polynomial and $x$ from the second,\n",
        "or choose $x^2$ from the first and the constant term from the second. Again:\n",
        "all pairs that hit the same target.\n",
        "\n",
        "## What to remember\n",
        "\n",
        "Convolution is the arithmetic of overlap.\n",
        "\n",
        "It takes two sources of contribution and asks, for every target, how many\n",
        "weighted ways the two sources can combine to hit that target. That is why the\n",
        "same formula counts dice sums, multiplies polynomials, adds independent random\n",
        "variables, blurs images, filters signals, and describes the memory of a linear\n",
        "system.\n",
        "\n",
        "The sliding picture is not a mnemonic pasted on afterward. It is the formula\n",
        "made visible: one list reversed because complements run backward, then shifted\n",
        "because the target changes.\n",
        "\n",
        "And the reason convolution keeps returning in serious mathematics is that\n",
        "Fourier and Laplace transforms know how to tame it. In the original world it is\n",
        "a sum over all overlapping histories. In the transformed world it is ordinary\n",
        "multiplication. A complicated memory becomes a simple product, provided you are\n",
        "willing to look at it in the right coordinates.\n",
        "\n",
        "## How to cite\n",
        "\n",
        "> Terry (2026). *Convolution: The Arithmetic of Overlap*. Retrieved from `https://terryfxh.github.io/math-notes/posts/convolution-the-arithmetic-of-overlap/`."
      ],
